<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">Maximum Likelihood and Maximum a Posteriori Estimation</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L39" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE)</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L788" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">MLE is a foundational method and therefore forms the backbone of many ML models. MLE provides a principle way of estimating unknown parameters of a probalistic model derived from observed data. In laymans terms, it is a statistical method used to estimate the parameters of a probability distribution or model. The goal of MLE is to find the set of parameters that makes the observed data as &quot;likely&quot; as possible, based on the model you're using. simpler terms, MLE helps you figure out which parameter values make your model fit the observed data best. In the case of the normal distribution, the MLE for \316\274 would turn out to be the sample mean of the data, and for \317\203, it would be the sample standard deviation.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L790" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L555" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Using Mohamads slides from optimization we take the following example:

We have N independent samples <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> from a distribution <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> which we wish to estimate by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> where <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEnJiM5NTI7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGMkY0">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEnJiM5NTI7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGMkY0</Equation> are the fixed parameters of the model we choose. The parameters are treated as random variables too, since we cannot exactly know their <Font italic="true">true</Font> values from data, we can only <Font italic="true">estimate </Font>them.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L557" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now, we assume a gaussion model <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> with unknown variance <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and unknown mean <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. Remember that variance describe how much the predictions change based on which subset of data is used, effectively the degree of sensivity of the models predictions due to changes in training data. Mean is used to calculate the average loss (error) on a dataset during model training.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L564" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The gaussian model can be expressed as:
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L562" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L571" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We want to fit a gaussian distribution for the data by estimating the parameters: </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L569" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
