<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L619" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Which of the following statements about likelihood are correct?

A. The likelihood is a probability distribution over parameters
B. The likelihood measures how probable the observed data are for a given parameter
C. The likelihood integrates to 1 over the parameter space
D. Maximizing likelihood is equivalent to maximizing log-likelihood

Correct answers: B and D

Explanation:
The likelihood evaluates how well a parameter explains fixed data, but it is not a probability distribution over parameters and does not integrate to one. The logarithm preserves maxima because it is monotonic.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
