<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 3" layout="Heading 3">Multiple Choice questions made by chat</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L800" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Q1
MAP estimation maximizes:

<Font encoding="UTF-8">A) The likelihood p(x\342\210\243\316\270)
B) The posterior probability p(\316\270\342\210\243x)
C) The prior probability p(\316\270)
</Font>D) The marginal likelihood p(x)

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
<Font encoding="UTF-8">MAP stands for Maximum A Posteriori. By definition, it selects the parameter value that maximizes the posterior distribution p(\316\270\342\210\243x).
</Font>
A is MLE, not MAP.
C ignores the data.
<Font encoding="UTF-8">D does not depend on \316\270 and cannot be maximized over parameters.
</Font>
Q2
Which term distinguishes MAP from MLE?

A) Likelihood
B) Posterior
C) Prior
D) Evidence

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Both MAP and MLE use the likelihood. The prior is the additional component introduced by MAP.

A appears in both methods.
B is the result, not the distinguishing ingredient.
D (evidence) is constant w.r.t. parameters.

Q3
MAP reduces to MLE when:

A) The data is Gaussian
B) The prior is uniform
C) The variance is known
D) The sample size is finite

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
A uniform prior contributes a constant to the log-posterior, which does not affect the argmax.
A is irrelevant; MAP works for any likelihood.
C does not remove the prior.
D is false; convergence happens as sample size grows, not merely because it is finite.

Q4
The MAP objective function is:

<Font encoding="UTF-8">A) logp(x\342\210\243\316\270)-logp(\316\270)
B) logp(x\342\210\243\316\270)+logp(\316\270)
C) p(x\342\210\243\316\270)p(\316\270)-p(x)
D) p(\316\270)/p(x\342\210\243\316\270)
</Font>
Correct answer: B

Explanation:
MAP maximizes the log-posterior, which equals the sum of the log-likelihood and log-prior.
A has the wrong sign.
<Font encoding="UTF-8">C and D do not correspond to Bayes\342\200\231 rule.
</Font>
Q5
A Gaussian prior on parameters leads to which form of regularization?

A) L1 regularization
B) L2 regularization
C) No regularization
D) Hard constraints

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
The log of a Gaussian prior produces a quadratic penalty, which is exactly L2 regularization.
A corresponds to a Laplace prior.
C occurs only with a flat prior.
D would require a degenerate prior.

Q6
Which estimator explicitly shrinks estimates toward a prior mean?

A) MLE
B) MAP
C) Ordinary least squares
D) Empirical risk minimization

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
MAP balances data evidence with the prior, causing shrinkage toward the prior mean.
MLE ignores priors entirely.
OLS is equivalent to MLE under Gaussian noise.
ERM is a general framework, not a specific estimator.

Q7
In MAP estimation, the marginal likelihood p(x):

A) Must be maximized
B) Must be estimated
C) Can be ignored for optimization
D) Must be differentiated

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
<Font encoding="UTF-8">p(x) does not depend on \316\270, so it does not affect the location of the maximum.
</Font>A is false; MAP does not optimize evidence.
B and D are unnecessary for MAP.

Q8
In MAP estimation, parameters are treated as:

A) Fixed but unknown constants
B) Random variables
C) Deterministic functions of data
D) Latent observations

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
MAP adopts a Bayesian viewpoint where parameters have probability distributions.
A describes MLE.
C is incorrect; MAP returns an estimate, not a function.
D confuses parameters with hidden variables.

Q9
Why can MAP be interpreted as regularized maximum likelihood?

A) The likelihood is modified
B) The prior adds a penalty term
C) The posterior is normalized
D) The evidence is minimized

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
The log-prior becomes a penalty term added to the log-likelihood.
A is false; likelihood remains unchanged.
C and D are unrelated to regularization.

Q10
Which prior results in L1 regularization?

A) Gaussian
B) Uniform
C) Laplace
D) Bernoulli

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
The Laplace prior leads to an absolute-value penalty, which is L1 regularization.
Gaussian gives L2.
Uniform gives no penalty.
Bernoulli is for discrete parameters.

Q11
<Font encoding="UTF-8">As the sample size n\342\206\222\342\210\236, the MAP estimator converges to:
</Font>
A) The prior mean
B) The posterior variance
C) The MLE
D) Zero

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
As data grows, the likelihood dominates the prior.
A occurs only if data is weak.
B is not an estimator.

D has no theoretical justification.

Q12
Which statement is false?

<Font encoding="UTF-8">A) MAP uses Bayes\342\200\231 theorem
</Font>B) MAP ignores the prior
C) MAP reduces overfitting
D) MAP generalizes MLE

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
MAP explicitly incorporates the prior.
A, C, and D are all true statements.

Q13
MAP estimation is particularly useful when:

A) Data is extremely abundant
B) No prior information exists
C) Data is scarce or noisy
D) The likelihood is uniform

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Priors help stabilize estimation when data is limited or unreliable.
A reduces the importance of priors.
<Font encoding="UTF-8">B contradicts MAP\342\200\231s purpose.
</Font>D makes inference impossible.

Q14
The MAP estimate corresponds to which statistic of the posterior?

A) Mean
B) Variance
C) Mode
D) Median

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
MAP finds the maximum of the posterior density, i.e., the mode.
A is the Bayesian posterior mean.
B is dispersion, not location.
D differs unless the posterior is symmetric.

Q15
MAP differs from full Bayesian inference because it:

A) Uses likelihoods
B) Uses priors
C) Produces a single point estimate
<Font encoding="UTF-8">D) Applies Bayes\342\200\231 theorem
</Font>
Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Full Bayesian inference retains the entire posterior distribution. MAP collapses it to a single best point.
A, B, and D are common to both.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
