<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">Cross-Validation</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L43" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Cross validation is used to check the performance of a ML model on unseen data while preventing overfitting. It works by splitting the dataset into several parts, training the model on some parts and testing it on the remaining parts, repeating the resampling process multiple times with different parts of the dataset (k-fold), and finally averaging the results from each validation step, to get average performance metrics.
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1052" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Several types of cross-validation exists, such as:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1053" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Holdout validation:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1054" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">A method where the split it typically 50-50 for training and testing. The biggest hurdle for this method is that only 50% of the data is used for training, risking important patterns being missed in the other half. The model in general has a high bias, resulting in possible underfitting</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1055" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1056" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Leave One Out Cross Validation (LOOCV):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1057" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">For this model, all data except a single datapoint is used for training, and is then repeated for every datapoint in the dataset. This ensures alle datapoint are used for training, leading to low bias. However, high variance can be made, if any datapoints are outliers. Lastly, this method is VERY computationally expensive, as it requires an iteration for each datapoint. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1058" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1059" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Stratified Cross Validation:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1060" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This method resembles K-fold cross validation, with the largest difference being that SCV keeps class proportions consistent in each fold. This ensures identical class distribution as the full dataset, making the training and test data resemble the full dataset the best.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1066" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The dataset is divided into k folds, with equal class compositions. In each iteration through the dataset, one fold is used for testing and the rest for training. The process is repeated k times, ensuring full dataset use. The model great for classification models, as it generalizes better by maintaining balanced class representation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1067" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1068" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">K-Fold Cross Validation:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1069" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">K-fold splits the dataset into equal sized folds, with no consideration for composition. The model is trained on k-1 folds and tested on the remaining fold, and iterated k times using different folds for testing, ensuring complete dataset coverage. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
