<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">multiple choice by chat:</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1086" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">Q1

What is the primary purpose of cross-validation?

A) To minimize training error
B) To estimate generalization (test) error
C) To maximize likelihood
D) To enforce regularization

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
Cross-validation is designed to estimate how well a model trained on a dataset will perform on unseen data.

A is incorrect because training error is computed directly on the training set.
C is unrelated to evaluation.
D is a modeling choice, not an evaluation method.

Q2

In K-fold cross-validation, the dataset is split into:

A) One training set and one test set
B) K disjoint subsets of equal size
C) K-1 training sets and one test set
D) K overlapping subsets

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
The data is partitioned into K non-overlapping subsets (folds), each used once for validation.

A describes a simple train\342\200\223test split.
C confuses folds with models.
D violates independence assumptions.

Q3

How many models are trained during K-fold cross-validation?

A) 1
B) K-1
C) K
D) n

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
One model is trained per fold, resulting in K trained models.

A is false because each fold produces a different model.
B miscounts the number of training rounds.
D corresponds to leave-one-out only if K = n.

Q4

What quantity does cross-validation estimate?

A) Training risk
B) Empirical risk on the full dataset
C) Expected test risk of the learning algorithm
D) Bayes risk

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Cross-validation approximates the expected generalization error of the algorithm.

A and B are optimistic estimates.
D requires knowledge of the true data-generating distribution.

Q5

Why is cross-validation error usually higher than training error?

A) Because fewer samples are used
B) Because models are undertrained
C) Because evaluation is performed on unseen data
D) Because the loss function changes

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Training error is optimistically biased. Validation error reflects performance on unseen data.

A is not the fundamental reason.
B is false; models are trained normally.
D is incorrect; the loss remains the same.

Q6

Which assumption is required for standard cross-validation to be valid?

A) Gaussian noise
B) Linear models
C) Independent and identically distributed samples
D) Convex loss functions

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Cross-validation assumes samples are i.i.d. Violations invalidate the estimate.

A, B, and D are not required.

Q7

Why is standard K-fold cross-validation inappropriate for time-series data?

A) Time-series models are non-linear
B) Observations are not independent
C) The loss cannot be computed
D) The dataset is too small

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
Time-series data has temporal dependence. Random splits cause information leakage.

A and D are irrelevant.
C is false.

Q8

What happens to bias and variance when K increases in K-fold cross-validation?

A) Bias increases, variance increases
B) Bias decreases, variance increases
C) Bias increases, variance decreases
D) Bias and variance both decrease

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
Larger K means larger training sets (lower bias) but smaller validation sets (higher variance).

Q9

Leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) corresponds to which value of K?

A) K = 2
B) K = log(n)
C) K = n
D) K = n/2

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Each data point is left out once as a validation set of size one.

Q10

Which statement about LOOCV is correct?

A) It has high bias and low variance
B) It has low bias and high variance
C) It underestimates test error
D) It requires a separate test set

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
LOOCV trains on nearly the full dataset (low bias) but has unstable validation errors (high variance).

Q11

Why is a separate test set still required after cross-validation-based model selection?

A) Cross-validation always overestimates error
B) Hyperparameter tuning introduces selection bias
C) Training error becomes meaningless
D) The likelihood changes

Correct answer: B

Explanation:
Using validation data to select models biases the estimated error downward.

Q12

Cross-validation primarily evaluates:

A) A fixed trained model
B) The Bayes-optimal classifier
C) The learning algorithm
D) The empirical distribution

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Cross-validation measures how the algorithm performs when retrained on different samples.

Q13

Which algorithm is most likely to exhibit high variance in cross-validation?

A) Linear regression
B) Ridge regression
C) Decision trees
D) Naive Bayes

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Decision trees are highly sensitive to training data, causing large variability across folds.

Q14

What is nested cross-validation used for?

A) Training multiple models in parallel
B) Estimating training error
C) Hyperparameter tuning with unbiased test error estimation
D) Reducing computational cost

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Nested CV separates model selection and evaluation to avoid optimistic bias.

Q15

Which of the following is a misuse of cross-validation?

A) Hyperparameter selection
B) Averaging validation errors
C) Applying it to non-i.i.d. data
D) Retraining on all data after tuning

Correct answer: C

Explanation:
Cross-validation assumes independence. Using it on dependent data yields misleading results.</Font></Text-field>
